#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""haring-pulse — each live worker's stream since the last pass, opening fast and
then at a pace the manager can retune while it runs.

    haring-pulse [project-dir] [--every SECONDS] [--first SECONDS]

project-dir defaults to the current directory, resolved to its git top level the same
way haring-worker resolves a workdir. --every is the gap between passes once something
has been reported, and defaults to 300. --first is the gap before that first report,
counted from the pass that first saw the worker, and defaults to 180: a worker that is
implementing a spec the manager has already written needs longer than that before what
it has to show is worth waking anyone for. Both take a positive integer and are held
between 60 and 300 seconds: a smaller number is used as 60, a larger as 300, and the
pulse says on a line starting `cadence` when it limited either one.

The pace can also be retuned mid-run, without stopping and restarting this, by writing
one number of seconds into <project>/.haring/cadence. The file belongs to one pulse:
this one removes any file left by an earlier pulse as it takes the project, reads the
file written while it runs between passes, and removes it on the way out. A number in
the file is held to the same 60 to 300 seconds, so a number outside the range is used
at the nearer edge rather than obeyed. A file that is not a positive whole number is
refused — the pace in force stands — while a missing or empty file means the --every
interval again. Each adoption, limit, clearing and refusal is printed on stdout as a
line starting `cadence`, once each when it happens, so a reader of this stream never
has to guess why the pace moved or failed to.

While it lives, this process's own pid is written to <project>/.haring/pulse, and the
file is removed on every way out — each exit above, a Ctrl-C, a kill — but only while it
still names this process. The cadence file is removed with it under the same condition.
One pulse watches a project. A second one reads the file before writing its own, and
when what it names is alive and is a haring-pulse it refuses: one line on standard error
naming that pid, and the pass interval in force when <project>/.haring/cadence holds a
positive number, named after limiting, then exit 1 with the file untouched. Every other
case takes the file over and runs — the file missing, unreadable, empty, the pid dead,
the pid alive without being a haring-pulse.
A pulse killed with SIGKILL leaves the file behind, because SIGKILL is the one death
nothing can be done on the way out of, which is why the reader checks the pid and the
command and not just the file: what it names being dead, or being no pulse, is no pulse.
The file naming a live haring-pulse is what a pulse watching means, and haring-worker
refuses to start a run into a project without it, which is what turns starting a pulse
from a rule the manager has to remember into something that answers back.

Meant to run under a tool that turns each stdout line into an event, where lines
printed within 200 milliseconds of each other arrive as one notification — so a whole
pass costs the manager one wake-up, however many lines it holds. Each pass prints, in
one burst, everything each live worker's event stream has added since the pass before:
the worker's thinking, its speech and its tool calls with their arguments, in the order
they happened, oldest first. The output of a call is never printed. The first pass on a
run reads its stream from where the run began; every pass after that reads only what
arrived since the one before, which is what makes the gap between passes cost nothing.

    <kind> <run-basename> <elapsed> <label> <text>

label is the tool's own name for a call, thinking or says for the worker's blocks, ask
when the worker has asked its manager a question, or api_retry when the endpoint is
refusing it, which is therefore announced without anything extra. A question does not
wait for a pass: it is surfaced the moment it lands, from the same one-second looking
that ends this when the workers end. A pass that has nothing new for a run still says
so once the run's last step is more than QUIET_AFTER seconds old: one line naming the
run as ever, saying the worker is thinking, how long since that step, how many thinking
tokens the run has accumulated, and how long since anything at all was written to
events.jsonl. That last age comes from the file's modification time, not from event
timestamps: the stream's thinking_tokens events carry no timestamp, and a worker
generating a long thought writes many of them without moving one, so a gap between
timestamped events says nothing about whether the worker is alive, and whether the file
is being written says everything. The elapsed times in the line grow, so no two of
these lines are the same — which is the point, because a worker deep in a long thought
must not read as one that has died. If the file has gone unwritten for longer
than SILENT_AFTER seconds, the line changes its words instead: the stream itself has
stopped, which a worker in a long thought never lets happen, and the worker may have
died. A run with nothing new for less than QUIET_AFTER is silent on that pass, which is
correct output here, not a fault.

A pass carrying more than MAX_CHARS characters of one run's stream prints the most
recent MAX_CHARS of them, behind one line saying how many characters were dropped. The
cap is a safety valve for a run that has gone wrong and never a way of trimming what a
worker did. The largest whole-run stream measured across 91 runs in this project is
286,081 characters, and MAX_CHARS sits well above it, while a pass normally carries
only the seconds since the pass before.

The workers it takes on are every worker live while it watches, whenever it started, so
a job that runs its independent work two at a time is covered from the first worker to
the last. It looks for them between passes rather than only when one is due, so it ends
within a second or two of the last one instead of at the next report.

Exits 0 once no worker it took on is live any more, or after about 120 seconds if no
worker ever appears at all — how long this waits for a first worker is what keeps a
pulse started against a project where nothing is ever launched from sitting there
forever. Exits 2 when called wrongly: an unknown flag, a project directory
that does not exist, or --every or --first given something the rules above refuse.
There is no way to be called rightly and still fail, except one: a haring-pulse
already watches this project, and one pulse to a project is the design — that
refusal exits 1.
"""
import sys, os, time, json, glob, subprocess, signal

USAGE = "usage: haring-pulse [project-dir] [--every SECONDS] [--first SECONDS]"
HARING = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)))
FIRST_LIVE_DEADLINE = 120  # a first worker appearing within this keeps the pulse alive
QUIET_AFTER = 60     # a run whose last step is this old has gone quiet and says so
# A first guess from two live observations, nothing firmer: a run whose events.jsonl
# has not been written for this long has stopped generating rather than thinking — a
# worker generating a thought writes to the file as it goes — though a worker running a
# long command also generates nothing while it waits, and one verification command
# alone ran 120 seconds tonight. The run's own stall guard in bin/haring-worker stops
# it after 900 seconds of silence, so this line exists to give the manager about
# twelve minutes of warning before the run stops itself.
SILENT_AFTER = 180
FLOOR = 60           # one minute is the shortest gap the manager may ever choose
CEILING = 300        # five minutes is the longest gap the manager may ever choose
FIRST_CEILING = 300  # an opening look is held to the same floor and ceiling
# A safety valve for a run that has gone wrong and never a way of trimming what a
# worker did. The largest whole-run stream measured across 91 runs in this project is
# 286,081 characters, and MAX_CHARS sits well above it, so the next slightly larger
# run does not fire it.
MAX_CHARS = 500000
CADENCE_FILE = "cadence"   # <project>/.haring/cadence, one number of seconds
PULSE_FILE = "pulse"       # <project>/.haring/pulse, the watching pulse's pid

# Turning the event stream into lines lives in lib/, shared with the other commands
# that read a run.
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(HARING, "lib"))
import haring_events


def die(message, code):
    print(message, file=sys.stderr)
    if code == 2:
        print(USAGE, file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(code)


def whole_seconds(flag, val):
    if not val.isdigit() or int(val) < 1:
        die(f"{flag} must be a positive integer, got: {val}", 2)
    return int(val)


def limited(flag, value, ceiling):
    """Return value inside the floor and ceiling, saying when it had to move."""
    if value < FLOOR:
        print(f"cadence {flag} {value}s is below the {FLOOR}s floor, using {FLOOR}s",
              flush=True)
        return FLOOR
    if value > ceiling:
        print(f"cadence {flag} {value}s is above the {ceiling}s ceiling, using {ceiling}s",
              flush=True)
        return ceiling
    return value


argv = sys.argv[1:]
project_arg, every, first = None, 300, 180
i = 0
while i < len(argv):
    a = argv[i]
    if a == "--every":
        i += 1
        if i >= len(argv):
            die("--every needs a number of seconds", 2)
        every = limited("--every", whole_seconds("--every", argv[i]), CEILING)
    elif a == "--first":
        i += 1
        if i >= len(argv):
            die("--first needs a number of seconds", 2)
        first = limited("--first", whole_seconds("--first", argv[i]), FIRST_CEILING)
    elif a.startswith("-") and a != "-":
        die(f"unknown option: {a}", 2)
    elif project_arg is None:
        project_arg = a
    else:
        die("haring-pulse takes at most one project directory", 2)
    i += 1

project_arg = project_arg or os.getcwd()
if not os.path.isdir(project_arg):
    die(f"not a directory: {project_arg}", 2)

# The project is the git top level of the given directory when there is one, else the
# directory itself — the same resolution haring-worker uses, so a pulse pointed at any
# subdirectory of a project finds the same .haring/runs/ a worker started there wrote to.
top = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", os.path.abspath(project_arg), "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"],
                     capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.strip()
project = top or os.path.abspath(project_arg)
cadence_file = os.path.join(project, ".haring", CADENCE_FILE)


def elapsed(since):
    s = int(time.time() - since)
    return f"{s // 60}m{s % 60:02d}s"


def alive(pid):
    try:
        os.kill(pid, 0)
        return True
    except OSError:
        return False


# Whether a pulse pid file names a pulse that is watching: the pid in it alive and its
# command a haring-pulse. A missing, unreadable or empty file, a dead pid, a live pid
# that is not a pulse — none of them is a pulse, and the caller takes the file over.
# Liveness is checked because SIGKILL leaves the file standing, and the command because
# the pid a dead pulse once held can be given to a different process.
def live_pulse_pid(path):
    try:
        text = open(path).read().strip()
    except OSError:
        return None
    if not text.isdigit() or not alive(int(text)):
        return None
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(["ps", "-p", text, "-o", "command="],
                             capture_output=True, text=True)
    except OSError:
        return None
    if out.returncode != 0 or "haring-pulse" not in out.stdout:
        return None
    return text


def landed(run):
    report = os.path.join(run, "report.json")
    return os.path.exists(report) and os.path.getsize(report) > 0


def live_runs():
    found = []
    for meta_path in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(project, ".haring", "runs", "*", "meta"))):
        run = os.path.dirname(meta_path)
        try:
            meta = dict(l.strip().split("=", 1) for l in open(meta_path) if "=" in l)
        except OSError:
            continue
        pid = meta.get("pid", "")
        if not haring_events.run_pid_alive(run, pid) or landed(run):
            continue
        found.append((run, meta, meta_path))
    return found


# One reader per run, kept for the life of this pulse: the reader remembers where it
# stopped in the run's stream, so each pass reads only what arrived since the pass
# before. The first pass on a run reads its stream from where the run began, with the
# same moment the worker began, taken from the meta file, as the zero of its clock.
readers = {}


# How far into each live run's ask.jsonl this pulse has read. A question is surfaced
# from the one-second looking between passes, not held for the next one, and the offset
# is what keeps a question that lands while a pass is being printed from being printed
# twice: it is consumed once, wherever the look that catches it falls.
ask_pos = {}


def surface_asks(runs):
    """Print each question these live runs have asked since the last look, at once.

    True when something was printed, so the caller counts the pace as reported. A line
    still being written is left for the next look, whole.
    """
    said = False
    for run, meta, meta_path in runs:
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(run, "ask.jsonl"), "rb") as f:
                f.seek(ask_pos.get(run, 0))
                fresh = f.read()
        except OSError:
            continue
        if not fresh.endswith(b"\n"):
            fresh = fresh[:fresh.rfind(b"\n") + 1]
        if not fresh:
            continue
        ask_pos[run] = ask_pos.get(run, 0) + len(fresh)
        prefix = (f"{meta.get('kind', '?')} {os.path.basename(run)} "
                  f"{elapsed(os.path.getmtime(meta_path))} ")
        for line in fresh.decode(errors="replace").splitlines():
            try:
                question = json.loads(line).get("content", "")
            except ValueError:
                question = line
            for row in (question.splitlines() or [""]):
                print(f"{prefix}ask {row}", flush=True)
        said = True
    return said


# The pace in force between passes, and the file content last refused. Refused content
# is remembered so one bad number is said once rather than on every pass until the file
# changes — the pulse's silence is its correctness, and a refusal is no exception.
pace = every
refused = None


def take_cadence():
    """Fold .haring/cadence into the pace, saying on stdout why a number was not obeyed.

    Called between passes. A number in the file becomes the pace from the next pass on,
    held to the 60 to 300 second range; nothing in the file means the --every interval
    again; anything else leaves the pace in force standing. The file lives where the
    manager writes and this reads, so the reader of the run lines cannot see it — which
    is why an adoption, a limit, a clearing and a refusal are all printed rather than
    left to be inferred from the timing of later lines.
    """
    global pace, refused
    try:
        text = open(cadence_file, errors="replace").read().strip()
    except OSError:
        text = ""
    if not text:
        # No file, or an empty one: the manager has taken the flag's interval back.
        refused = None
        if pace != every:
            pace = every
            print(f"cadence .haring/cadence cleared, back to --every {every}s", flush=True)
    elif not text.isdigit() or int(text) < 1:
        if text != refused:
            refused = text
            print(f"cadence {text!r} refused, not a number of seconds", flush=True)
    else:
        refused = None
        number, edge = int(text), None
        if number < FLOOR:
            number, edge = FLOOR, f"below the {FLOOR}s floor"
        elif number > CEILING:
            number, edge = CEILING, f"above the {CEILING}s ceiling"
        if number != pace:
            pace = number
            said = f"cadence .haring/cadence says {int(text)}s"
            if edge:
                said += f", {edge}"
            print(f"{said}, --every said {every}s, using {pace}s", flush=True)


# The pid file is the other side of haring-worker's refusal to start a run nobody is
# watching: while it names this process, a run may start, and once it does not, none may.
# It goes down after every check above has passed, so a call refused for a bad flag or a
# missing project leaves no file behind. A second pulse refused here leaves it standing
# too: the refusal happens before anything is written, so the first pulse's claim on the
# file is exactly what the second one read. The refusal names the cadence number only
# when the file holds a positive number, named after limiting to the floor and ceiling —
# anything else is not the interval in force and is not claimed. The removal
# sits in a finally, so every exit in the loop below lands there; the handler turns a
# kill into an exit so a killed pulse reaches it as well, since the default action for
# SIGTERM stops the process without unwinding. The removal reads the file first, so it
# takes down only a file that names this process. SIGKILL is the one death nothing can
# be done on the way out of, which is why the reader of this file is made to check the
# pid and the command, not just the file.
os.makedirs(os.path.join(project, ".haring"), exist_ok=True)
pulse_file = os.path.join(project, ".haring", PULSE_FILE)
holder = live_pulse_pid(pulse_file)
if holder:
    message = f"a haring-pulse (pid {holder}) is already watching this project"
    try:
        cadence = open(cadence_file).read().strip()
    except OSError:
        cadence = ""
    if cadence.isdigit() and int(cadence) >= 1:
        in_force = min(max(int(cadence), FLOOR), CEILING)
        message += f", at a pass interval of {in_force}s"
    die(message, 1)
with open(pulse_file, "w") as f:
    f.write(f"{os.getpid()}\n")

# The cadence belongs to the pulse that reads it. A file left by an earlier pulse is
# removed now, once the pid file is ours, and said on stdout so its number is not
# mistaken for one this pulse chose. A second pulse refused above never reaches here,
# so it touches neither file.
try:
    with open(cadence_file, errors="replace") as f:
        stale = f.read().strip()
except OSError:
    stale = None
if stale is not None:
    try:
        os.remove(cadence_file)
    except OSError:
        pass
    print(f"cadence removed a .haring/cadence left from an earlier pulse "
          f"(it said {stale}); --every {every}s stands", flush=True)


def take_pulse_down():
    """Remove the pid file and the cadence, but only while the pid file names this process."""
    try:
        with open(pulse_file) as f:
            text = f.read().strip()
    except OSError:
        text = ""
    if text and text != str(os.getpid()):
        return   # another pulse holds the file now, and needs it standing
    for path in (pulse_file, cadence_file):
        try:
            os.remove(path)
        except OSError:
            pass


def leave(signum, frame):
    sys.exit(0)   # into the finally, which is where the pid file is removed


signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, leave)

try:
    start = time.time()
    ever_live = False
    # Nothing has been reported yet, so the opening interval still governs: a worker found
    # here has a stream already going, and waiting a whole long gap to read it is what
    # makes the first words arrive after the route they name has already been paid for.
    # The file may shorten the opening look but never lengthen it.
    reported = False
    while True:
        runs = live_runs()
        if runs:
            ever_live = True
            # Every worker live on a pass is taken on, whenever it started, so a job
            # that runs its independent work two at a time is covered to the last one.
            # The exit checks against the live set below follow from that: this ends
            # when nothing it would have taken on is left.
            for run, meta, meta_path in runs:
                reader = readers.get(run)
                if reader is None:
                    reader = haring_events.RunReader(when_ts=os.path.getmtime(meta_path))
                    readers[run] = reader
                events = os.path.join(run, "events.jsonl")
                lines = reader.read(events) if os.path.exists(events) else []
                prefix = (f"{meta.get('kind', '?')} {os.path.basename(run)} "
                          f"{elapsed(os.path.getmtime(meta_path))} ")
                # The characters counted are the stream's own, not the prefixed rows
                # printed around them — the same measure the largest whole run was
                # taken with.
                total = sum(len(text) for _, text, _ in lines)
                if total > MAX_CHARS:
                    kept, held = [], 0
                    for entry in reversed(lines):
                        if held + len(entry[1]) > MAX_CHARS:
                            break
                        kept.append(entry)
                        held += len(entry[1])
                    kept.reverse()
                    print(f"{prefix}{total - held} earlier characters dropped",
                          flush=True)
                    reported = True
                else:
                    kept = lines
                for label, text, _ in kept:
                    # A block arrives as one entry carrying the whole block, its lines
                    # joined with newlines; the pass prints one line per line of it. An
                    # entry with no text at all — a report handover names its tool and
                    # nothing else — still prints its one line.
                    for row in (text.splitlines() or [""]):
                        print(f"{prefix}{label} {row}", flush=True)
                        reported = True
                if not lines and time.time() - reader.when >= QUIET_AFTER:
                    # Nothing new is not the same as nothing to say: a worker deep in a
                    # long thought looks, from here, like a dead one, and this is what
                    # keeps the two apart. The elapsed times in each line grow, so each
                    # pass's line is new under the rule every line here answers to. The
                    # age of the file comes from its modification time, because the
                    # thinking_tokens a generating worker writes carry no timestamp.
                    written = (os.path.getmtime(events) if os.path.exists(events)
                               else os.path.getmtime(meta_path))
                    if time.time() - written >= SILENT_AFTER:
                        print(f"{prefix}nothing written to events.jsonl for "
                              f"{elapsed(written)}, the worker may have died",
                              flush=True)
                    else:
                        print(f"{prefix}thinking, no step for {elapsed(reader.when)}, "
                              f"{reader.thinking_tokens:,} thinking tokens, "
                              f"events.jsonl written {elapsed(written)} ago",
                              flush=True)
                    reported = True
            # The pass above took a moment to print; anything asked while it printed is
            # caught here rather than held until the next look a whole gap later.
            if surface_asks(runs):
                reported = True
        if ever_live:
            if not runs:
                sys.exit(0)
        elif time.time() - start >= FIRST_LIVE_DEADLINE:
            sys.exit(0)

        take_cadence()

        if not ever_live:
            # Look again every second until the first worker appears, rather than sleeping a
            # whole interval. A caller may start this a moment before it starts a worker, and
            # on the default five-minute interval that worker would otherwise never be seen:
            # the deadline above would end the run first.
            time.sleep(1)
            continue
        gap = pace if reported else min(first, pace)
        # The gap decides when the next line is due, not when this looks: a worker that ends
        # mid-gap ends the pulse within a tick, since otherwise the last worker of a job
        # leaves this running against an empty project for up to a whole interval.
        deadline = time.time() + gap
        while time.time() < deadline:
            time.sleep(1)
            now_live = live_runs()
            if surface_asks(now_live):
                reported = True
            if not now_live:
                sys.exit(0)
finally:
    take_pulse_down()
